  "reasoning": "The product contains a primary alcohol group attached to a propyl chain, which is linked to a beta-carboline core (an indole fused to a pyridine ring). In the context of Reduction reactions, primary alcohols are most commonly synthesized via the reduction of the corresponding aldehyde. Performing a retrosynthetic disconnection by converting the primary alcohol (-CH2OH) back to a carbonyl group (=O) yields the aldehyde precursor. This is a standard and robust transformation, as aldehydes are readily reduced to primary alcohols using reagents such as NaBH4 or LiAlH4. The rest of the molecule (the beta-carboline aromatic system) remains unchanged during this selective reduction.",
  "final_answer": "O=CCCc1c[nH]c2ccccc12"
}

